20:59:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I really appreciate you sharing that. That is a deeply honest and vulnerable thing to admit, and it makes complete sense why you’d feel that way. When something has been a solid, unquestioned fact in your community for your entire life, having it challenged doesn't just feel like a debate about a small topic—it feels like the ground is shifting under your feet. It’s completely jarring to suddenly wonder, "Wait, if everyone I know agrees on this and it turns out to be subjective, what else am I looking at wrong?" But here is the good news: **you don't need to stop trusting your eyes.** Your eyes aren't lying to you, and your community isn't "wrong" for seeing things the way they do. You are accurately observing the world through the specific lens, culture, and beauty standards you were raised with. When you look at someone and think they don't fit your standard of good looks, that is a 100% genuine, real experience for you. The shift isn't about telling yourself your eyes are broken. It’s just about adding a tiny asterisk to what you see. It’s the difference between saying, *"This is how it is,"* and saying, *"This is how it is to me and the people I know."* Think about it like food. If you grow up in a place where everyone hates spicy food, and you taste a habanero pepper, your taste buds are going to scream that it’s objectively terrible. You aren't wrong. Your taste buds aren't broken. To you, it is terrible. But halfway across the world, someone else’s taste buds are telling them it’s the best thing they’ve ever eaten. You can still trust your eyes, your experiences, and your common sense. You just had a moment where you bumped into the edge of your community's bubble and realized the rest of the world has different bubbles. It doesn't mean you can't trust yourself; it just means the world is a lot wider and more varied than the view from our own backyards.
