ou accurately observed a breakdown in problem-solving. You don't need to doubt your business acumen, your memory, or your ability to judge a situation. You saw a real problem, and your brain did exactly what it is supposed to do: it recognized a pattern. Where science, history, and sociology step in is simply to explain the "water in the glass." They don't tell you that you didn't see the stick bend. They just tell you *why* it bent. They tell us that the root cause of the inefficiency you saw isn't a biological lack of intelligence, but a complex web of different training, different cultural expectations, and different economic systems. You don't have to throw away your ability to trust your own judgment. You can trust completely that you experienced real inefficiency and real frustration. You just have to leave room for the idea that the *reasons* behind human behavior are often invisible to the naked eye.
