21:11:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It makes complete sense that it feels this way. Human beings are literally wired to do exactly what you are describing: we are built to size things up quickly, recognize patterns, and categorize the world around us. It is a survival instinct. When you see a pattern with your own eyes, and your peers see the same pattern, your brain naturally concludes that you have found a simple, fundamental truth. In our day-to-day lives, relying on that kind of "common sense" usually works. But the reason we need science—what you are calling "artificial overthinking"—is because human "common sense" is actually notoriously bad at understanding complex, invisible realities. For thousands of years, human common sense dictated that the Earth was flat. Anyone could look outside, size up the horizon, and see that it was flat. Common sense also dictated that the sun revolved around the Earth—you could literally watch it move across the sky every day. If someone came along with complex mathematical theories about gravity and orbits, it sounded like they were artificially overthinking a very simple, obvious reality. But the "overthinking" was right, and common sense was wrong. The same thing applies to human biology. When we look at people from different cultures who act differently, solve problems differently, or have different levels of technological development, our "common sense" instinct is to say, *“They are fundamentally, biologically different.”* But when we actually look at the DNA—when we use the scientific tools that let us see past the surface—we find that they aren't. It is absolutely true that *individuals* have different levels of natural intelligence. You will find naturally brilliant people and naturally slower people in every single town, city, and country on Earth. But when you zoom out to entire *populations* or nationalities, those biological differences even out. There is no genetic evidence that one nationality is born with a higher capacity for intelligence than another. I know it feels like I am throwing a textbook at a simple observation. But the reason I rely on the science isn't to overcomplicate things just to be difficult. It is because our natural instinct to "size people up" based on their group has historically led us to the wrong conclusions about human biology. Science is the tool we use to make sure our eyes aren't deceiving us.
